UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 33, Lesson 3: Noun groups', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Noun groups package a thing with pointers and describers (the careful young storyteller); building them precisely helps information and narrative writing.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nBuild a noun group around storyteller with two describers and a pointer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nExample: that patient Year 4 storyteller.\n\nCHECK\nPiling random adjectives that contradict each other weakens the group.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite eight expanded noun groups. Label pointer/describer/thing in four and use three in a respectful protocol paragraph.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Noun groups package a thing with pointers and describers (the careful young storyteller); building them precisely helps information and narrative writing.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Language and spelling","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-VOCAB-01","EN2-SPELL-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write eight expanded noun groups. Label pointer/describer/thing in four and use three in a respectful protocol paragraph. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Example: that patient Year 4 storyteller. Reasoning: I used noun group by attending to describer rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Piling random adjectives that contradict each other weakens the group. Correction: Example: that patient Year 4 storyteller. Why: the correction restores accurate use of noun group.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Piling random adjectives that contradict each other weakens the group. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating noun group on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with noun group, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Example: that patient Year 4 storyteller. This matches the lesson skill for noun group.","options":["Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Example: that patient Year 4 storyteller.","Piling random adjectives that contradict each other weakens the group."],"question":"Build a noun group around storyteller with two describers and a pointer. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Piling random adjectives that contradict each other weakens the group. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of describer with clear evidence.","Piling random adjectives that contradict each other weakens the group.","Noun groups package a thing with pointers and describers (the careful young storyteller); building them precisely helps information and narrative writing.","Example: that patient Year 4 storyteller."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising noun group?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named describer, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the describer used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact."],"question":"A learner is practising noun group. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries noun group.","options":["Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event.","Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Piling random adjectives that contradict each other weakens the group.","Point to exact words in the sentence that show noun group and say what they do."],"question":"Read: \"Beside the oval, Kai practised bowling until the sun slipped low.\" Which choice best uses noun group from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Example: that patient Year 4 storyteller.","Piling random adjectives that contradict each other weakens the group."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about noun group?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise describer?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess.","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about noun group.","Retype the worked model as the final answer.","Change fonts and claim the work is new.","Piling random adjectives that contradict each other weakens the group."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of noun group to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating noun group on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Example: that patient Year 4 storyteller. Reasoning: I used noun group by attending to describer rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with noun group, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Piling random adjectives that contradict each other weakens the group. Correction: Example: that patient Year 4 storyteller. Why: the correction restores accurate use of noun group.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y4-w33-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise noun group.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use noun group with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify describer and explain why it matters.","I can practise pointer independently on a new prompt.","I can notice thing needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Noun groups package a thing with pointers and describers (the careful young storyteller); building them precisely helps information and narrative writing. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Noun groups package a thing with pointers and describers (the careful young storyteller); building them precisely helps information and narrative writing."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about noun group?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"The approved classroom picture book\" = pointer + describers + thing. Expanding noun groups adds detail without a new sentence every time.\n\nTRY FIRST\nBuild a noun group around storyteller with two describers and a pointer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nExample: that patient Year 4 storyteller.","explain":"Noun groups package a thing with pointers and describers (the careful young storyteller); building them precisely helps information and narrative writing.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Piling random adjectives that contradict each other weakens the group. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Building precise noun groups.","caption":"Detail without extra sentences.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Noun group</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">the approved picture book</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Parts</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">pointer + describers + thing</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: \"The approved classroom picture book\" = pointer + describers + thing. Expanding noun groups adds detail without a new se Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: \"The approved classroom picture book\" = pointer + describers + thing. Expanding noun groups adds detail without a new se Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no explanation.","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about noun group?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"The approved classroom picture book\" = pointer + describers + thing. Expanding noun groups adds detail without a new sentence every time. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Useful expansion vs cluttered contradicting words.","caption":"Use groups in a protocol paragraph.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Expand</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">patient Year 4 storyteller</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Weak</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">contradictory adjectives</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no explanation.","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about noun group?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPiling random adjectives that contradict each other weakens the group. Here is a more productive model: \"The approved classroom picture book\" = pointer + describers + thing. Expanding noun groups adds detail without a new sentence every time.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nExample: that patient Year 4 storyteller.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\